    summary_en: "The EU Cyber Resilience Act (CRA) has officially taken effect, setting minimum cybersecurity standards for connected products placed on the EU market. Under the Act, all products containing digital components must comply with the cybersecurity requirements stipulated by the CRA by the end of 2027. The CRA aims to enhance product information transparency and integrate minimum security requirements into the existing CE marking system. Furthermore, EU manufacturers must proactively report exploited vulnerabilities and serious security incidents to relevant authorities (such as the BSI's CSIRT) starting in September 2026. The German Federal Office for Information Security (BSI) announced that, leveraging its experience in critical infrastructure security and standardization, it is actively seeking to take over national market regulatory responsibilities under the CRA framework. The BSI has issued technical guidance TR-03183 and is assisting manufacturers in preparing in advance, and raising industry awareness and readiness for the CRA, through existing mechanisms such as its \"IT-Sicherheitskennzeichen\".",
